values
  ('m1-what-is-a-product', 2, 1, 'The Product & the PM', 1, 'What is a product?',
   null, 9,
   $aiwjson$[{"type":"slides","title":"What is a product?","slides":[{"n":1,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":""},{"n":2,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"What is a Product?\nFoundations of Product Management | Module 1 | Topic 1\nAI–enabled Product Management Program"},{"n":3,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":""},{"n":4,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":""},{"n":5,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"STORY ONE — 2010\nBurbn had ten features.\nInstagram kept one.\nWhat if the feature everyone ignores is the only one that matters?"},{"n":6,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":""},{"n":7,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"STORY TWO — 2007\nNo app. Just three air mattresses.\nWhat does it take to know a need is real — before you've built anything at all?"},{"n":8,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":""},{"n":9,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"STORY THREE — 2012\nThe game failed.\nThe side tool didn't.\nWhat if the product you're building isn't the one you end up shipping?"},{"n":10,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"Three stories. One pattern.\nInstagram\nCut down to the one thing users actually loved.\nAirbnb\nTested a real need before writing any code.\nSlack\nNoticed the tool people used daily, unsold."},{"n":11,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"Three stories. One pattern.\nInstagram\nCut down to the one thing users actually loved.\nAirbnb\nTested a real need before writing any code.\nSlack\nNoticed the tool people used daily, unsold.\nNone of these products were invented in a meeting room. What if the best ones are never invented at all — only noticed?"},{"n":12,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"PRODUCT = SOLUTION"},{"n":13,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"Problem\nPRODUCT = SOLUTION"},{"n":14,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"Problem\nTHE PROBLEM YOU CAN SAY\nPRODUCT = SOLUTION"},{"n":15,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"Problem\nTHE PROBLEM YOU CAN SAY\n\"I need a place to sleep tonight.\"\nPRODUCT = SOLUTION"},{"n":16,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"PRODUCT = SOLUTION\nProblem\nTHE PROBLEM YOU CAN SAY\nTHE PROBLEM YOU POINT AT\n\"I need a place to sleep tonight.\""},{"n":17,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"Problem\nTHE PROBLEM YOU CAN SAY\nTHE PROBLEM YOU POINT AT\n\"I need a place to sleep tonight.\"\n\"I've rearranged my desk three times today. Still doesn't feel right.\"\nPRODUCT = SOLUTION"},{"n":18,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"“A product is anything that can be offered to a market for attention, acquisition, use, or consumption that might satisfy a need or want. It includes physical objects, services, persons, place, organizations or ideas.”” — Philip Kotler & Kevin Lane Keller, Marketing management\nDeﬁnition of Product by the Book\n“Need-satisfying offering” — McCarthy & Perreault\n“A product is a set of tangible and intangible attributes, including packaging, colour, price, manufacturer’s prestige, retailer’s prestige, and manufacturer’s and retailer’s services, which the buyer may accept as offering want-satisfaction.” — William J. Stanton, Fundamentals of Marketing"},{"n":19,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"Product\nPhysical\nDigital\nService\nA combination"},{"n":20,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"A product is not what you build."},{"n":21,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"A product is not what you build.\nIt's what people can't stop using."},{"n":22,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"Whatʼs a successful product?"},{"n":23,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"“A successful product changes some aspect of user behavior — even if only how, where, or how often they do something they were already doing.\"”\nWhatʼs a successful product?"},{"n":24,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"What problem have you walked past today, without noticing?"}]}]$aiwjson$::jsonb,
   'I have gone through all the slides in this lesson and understood them.', null, 'module-1/topic-1/m1-t1-st1-what-is-a-product.pdf', 'M1_T1_ST1_ What Is a Product_.pptx (2).pdf', 'e469a718041880be9ea77a8395e15bdd708c05c794e81cb22517094f8ca3133a'),
  ('m1-product-vs-engineering', 2, 1, 'The Product & the PM', 2, 'Dimensions of product and difference between product and engineering',
   'Same team. Same sprint. Two completely different questions.', 9,
   $aiwjson$[{"type":"slides","title":"Dimensions of product and difference between product and engineering","slides":[{"n":1,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":""},{"n":2,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"Product vs Engineering\nSame team. Same sprint. Two completely different questions.\nFoundations of Product Management | Module 1 | Topic 2\nAI–enabled Product Management Program"},{"n":3,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"One question builds it right.\nThe other asks if it should exist."},{"n":4,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"One question builds it right.\nThe other asks if it should exist.\nENGINEERING ASKS\n\"Can we build this well?\"\nPRODUCT ASKS\n\"Should we build this at all?\""},{"n":5,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"One question builds it right.\nThe other asks if it should exist.\nENGINEERING ASKS\n\"Can we build this well?\"\nPRODUCT ASKS\n\"Should we build this at all?\"\nNeither question is smaller. They live in different rooms of the same house."},{"n":6,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"THE CLASSIC PM LENS\nWhere does a product actually live?\nDESIRABILITY\nFEASIBILITY\nVIABILITY\nPRODUCT\nDesirability, feasibility, viability — the three lenses of design thinking (Tim Brown, IDEO, \"Change by Design,\" 2009)."},{"n":7,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"THE CLASSIC PM LENS\nWhere does a product actually live?\nDESIRABILITY\nDoes anyone want it?\nFEASIBILITY\nCan we actually build it?\nVIABILITY\nCan it sustain a business?\nPRODUCT\nDesirability, feasibility, viability — the three lenses of design thinking (Tim Brown, IDEO, \"Change by Design,\" 2009)."},{"n":8,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"THE CLASSIC PM LENS\nWhere does a product actually live?\nDESIRABILITY\nDoes anyone want it?\nFEASIBILITY\nCan we actually build it?\nVIABILITY\nCan it sustain a business?\nPRODUCT\nDesirability, feasibility, viability — the three lenses of design thinking (Tim Brown, IDEO, \"Change by Design,\" 2009).\nUser\nBusiness\nEngineering"},{"n":9,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"THE CLASSIC PM LENS\nWhere does a product actually live?\nDESIRABILITY\nDoes anyone want it?\nFEASIBILITY\nCan we actually build it?\nVIABILITY\nCan it sustain a business?\nPRODUCT\nDesirability, feasibility, viability — the three lenses of design thinking (Tim Brown, IDEO, \"Change by Design,\" 2009).\nMiss one circle, and it's not a product yet.\nUser\nBusiness\nEngineering"},{"n":10,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"you build something — and there's no one to buy it.\nyou have buyers and the idea — but no money to fund it.\nyou have buyers and the money — but no tech to build it.\nImagine"},{"n":11,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"2015 · MISSING FEASIBILITY\nImagine having eager buyers and all the funding — but the technology just won't work.\n$9B peak valuation, on a machine that never worked\nDesirability\nFeasibility\nViability"},{"n":12,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"2014 · MISSING DESIRABILITY\nImagine building something — and there's no one to buy it.\n$170M written off within a year of launch\nDesirability\nFeasibility\nViability"},{"n":13,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"2014 · MISSING DESIRABILITY\nImagine building something — and there's no one to buy it.\n$170M written off within a year of launch\nDesirability\nFeasibility\nViability"},{"n":14,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"2020 · MISSING VIABILITY\nImagine having the money, the stars, and the app — and still, no one stays.\n$1.75B raised — shut down six months after launch\nDesirability\nFeasibility\nViability"},{"n":15,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":""},{"n":16,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"ALL THREE, AT ONCE\nNow picture the one that hit every circle at the same time.\nFree · Instant · Everywhere one payments rail, shared by every bank\nDesirability\nFeasibility\nViability"},{"n":17,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"TEST YOURSELF\nWhich circle is missing?\nA fitness app with flawless engineering and a fully funded roadmap. Users open it once — and never come back.\nInvestors are lined up. Thousands of people are on the waitlist. The core technology doesn't exist yet.\nEveryone loves it. It works perfectly. It costs three times more to make than anyone will pay for it.\n1\n2\n3"},{"n":18,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"A fitness app with flawless engineering and a fully funded roadmap. Users open it once — and never come back.\nInvestors are lined up. Thousands of people are on the waitlist. The core technology doesn't exist yet.\nEveryone loves it. It works perfectly. It costs three times more to make than anyone will pay for it.\n1\n2\n3\nDesirability\nGreat build, never opened again.\nFeasibility\nDemand and funding, nothing to ship.\nViability\nLoved and functional — unaffordable to make.\nTHE REVEAL\nHere's what each one was missing."},{"n":19,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"Engineering makes one circle stronger.\nProduct decides which idea deserves the middle of all three.\nMakes the feasible circle wider and stronger.\nDecides which ideas deserve all three."},{"n":20,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"A SHIFT ALREADY UNDERWAY\nAI is quietly redrawing these three circles."},{"n":21,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"A SHIFT ALREADY UNDERWAY\nAI is quietly redrawing these three circles.\nDESIRABILITY\nFEASIBILITY\nVIABILITY"},{"n":22,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"A SHIFT ALREADY UNDERWAY\nAI is quietly redrawing these three circles.\nDESIRABILITY\nAI simulates users before you build a thing.\nFEASIBILITY\nWhat took a sprint now takes an afternoon.\nVIABILITY\nAI models pricing and demand before you launch.\nThe circles haven't changed. How fast you can test each one has."},{"n":23,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"It's not enough to be wanted. Or buildable. Or fundable.\nA product is all three, at once."}]}]$aiwjson$::jsonb,
   'I have gone through all the slides in this lesson and understood them.', null, 'module-1/topic-1/m1-t1-st2-product-vs-engineering.pdf', 'M1_T1_ST2_ Product vs Engineering — Inside the Three Circles.pptx.pdf', '1c5cc8920dab7d063c4d60bf80b9cc40de0f37bda101550a9eb0bc81ea923964'),
  ('m1-who-is-a-pm', 2, 1, 'The Product & the PM', 3, 'Who is a PM — and who isn''t',
   'Not the boss. Not the coder. Then who?', 5,
   $aiwjson$[{"type":"slides","title":"Who is a PM — and who isn't","slides":[{"n":1,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":""},{"n":2,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"Who Is a Product Manager?\nNot the boss. Not the coder. Then who?\nFoundations of Product Management | Module 1 | Topic 3\nAI–enabled Product Management Program"},{"n":3,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"A PHRASE EVERYONE'S HEARD\n\"The CEO of the product.\"\nIt's the line every PM eventually hears — and the one every good PM eventually has to unlearn. A CEO hires. A CEO fires. A CEO can say yes without asking anyone. A product manager can do none of that.\nSo if a PM isn't the CEO of anything — what are they actually in charge of?"},{"n":4,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"Three things a PM is often mistaken for.\nTHE BOSS\nNo one on the engineering or design team reports to them.\nTHE TRACKER\nUpdating a spreadsheet of due dates isn't the job — it's a symptom of missing it.\nA PART-TIME CODER\nThey don't own the codebase — and the best ones know better than to touch it."},{"n":5,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"Job titles people confuse with product manager.\nPROJECT MANAGER\nTracks timelines across teams. A\nPM decides what's worth tracking.\nPRODUCT OWNER\nManages the backlog for one team.\nA PM decides what belongs on it.\nPRODUCT MARKETING\nTells the world why it matters. A PM decides what it actually is.\nBUSINESS ANALYST\nDocuments how a process works today. A PM decides how it works tomorrow.\nEach of these is a real, valuable job. None of them is this one."},{"n":6,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"So what's actually theirs?\nDESIGN\nOwns the interface\nENGINEERING\nOwns the code\nBUSINESS\nOwns the deal\nPRODUCT\nMANAGER\nNot the edges. All of the connective tissue in between."},{"n":7,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"AMAZON, SINCE 2004\nThe product manager writes the press release — before a single line of code exists.\nAmazon\nStep one: the PR/FAQ not the roadmap, not the spec — a fake news article for a product that doesn't exist yet\nIf the press release isn't exciting, the idea isn't ready to build. The PM's first deliverable isn't code. It's clarity."},{"n":8,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"GOOGLE, 2009\nA product leader tested 41 shades of blue — and it worked.\nGoogle\n~$200M/yr reported revenue lift, from picking one shade over another\nNot one designer's favorite blue, and not the PM's opinion either. Just a decision about what to test — and the discipline to let the data decide the rest."},{"n":9,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"One day, seven diﬀerent hats.\nListening\nMeasuring\nAligning\nUnblocking\nDeciding\nShipping\nNone of these is the whole job. All seven, most days, are.\nMonitoring"},{"n":10,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"THE ORIGINAL DEFINITION, 1997\n\"A good product manager is the CEO of the product.\"\nThat's the line from Ben Horowitz's Good Product Manager, Bad Product Manager, written at Netscape and still handed to new PMs today. He didn't mean the org chart. He meant something narrower and harder: take full responsibility for whether the product succeeds — with none of a CEO's actual authority to make it happen.\nBen Horowitz & David Weiden, \"Good Product Manager, Bad Product Manager,\" Netscape, 1997."},{"n":11,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"Expertise here isn't one skill. It's four.\nMINDSET\nHow you think — curiosity and evidence over opinion.\nPROCESSES\nHow you work — repeatable ways to explore an idea.\nSTRATEGY\nWhere you point it — which few problems are worth solving.\nFRAMEWORK\nWhat you reach for — so judgment isn't guesswork.\nNone of the four replace the others. Together, they're what makes it repeatable, not accidental."},{"n":12,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"A product manager owns no code, no design, no team — and all of the outcome.\nWhich part would you want to own?"}]}]$aiwjson$::jsonb,
   'I have gone through all the slides in this lesson and understood them.', null, 'module-1/topic-1/m1-t1-st3-who-is-a-pm.pdf', 'M1_T1_ST3_ Who Is a Product Manager.pptx.pdf', '92372277fb52438124de41d42b7643d24c112960bd066f0dd4178b35b47f4ddd'),
  ('m1-mindset-of-a-pm', 2, 1, 'The Product & the PM', 4, 'Mindset of a PM',
   'It was never just about building. Or designing. Then what is it about?', 5,
   $aiwjson$[{"type":"slides","title":"Mindset of a PM","slides":[{"n":1,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":""},{"n":2,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"The Mindset of a PM\nIt was never just about building. Or designing. Then what is it about?\nFoundations of Product Management | Module 1 | Topic 4\nAI–enabled Product Management Program"},{"n":3,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"LETTING EVIDENCE OUTRANK OPINION — ONGOING\nRight now, one booking site is running over a thousand experiments on the same page you'd be looking at.\nBooking.com built its culture on this: almost no feature ships from a strong opinion alone. It ships from a test — two versions, live, in front of real users, and the data decides which one the whole site keeps.\nEven a senior executive's favorite idea can lose to a smaller team's variant, if the numbers say so.\nVariant A\nVariant B\nThe opinion in the room is a hypothesis. The user's behavior is the answer."},{"n":4,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"THE SCOPE OF THE JOB\nSix disciplines feed the decision. A PM masters none of them alone — and answers for all of them together.\nEngineering\nWhat's feasible, and at what cost\nDesign\nHow it should feel to use\nUser research\nWhat the user actually needs\nMarketing & branding\nWhy it deserves attention\nData & analytics\nWhat's actually working\nBusiness strategy\nWhy it's worth the company's bet\nNot a specialist in six fields. A translator who can stand in each room and still make one call."},{"n":5,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"You can learn to wireframe a screen in a weekend. You can learn to ship code in a month.\nNeither one teaches you to ask the question that actually matters: is this worth building at all?\nSee through the user's eyes\nKnow who you're really for\nOwn a space in their mind\nWatch where value moves\nSell a belief, not a spec sheet\nLet evidence outrank opinion"},{"n":6,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"SELLING A BELIEF, NOT A SPEC SHEET — 1997\nThe ad ran for a minute. It never once showed the product.\nIn 1997, a struggling Apple ran \"Think Different\" — black-and-white portraits of Einstein, Gandhi, Picasso, Amelia Earhart. No laptop. No spec. Just a line: the people who are crazy enough to think they can change the world are the ones who do.\nIt sold a belief about who you'd be if you bought in — and let the product earn the rest later.\n\"Here's to the crazy ones.\" — zero product shots.\nBranding is the promise. Marketing is keeping it in front of the right people."},{"n":7,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"OWNING A SPACE IN SOMEONE'S HEAD — 1968\nA soft-drink brand couldn't out-cola Coke. So it stopped trying to be a cola at all.\n7 Up was a distant, forgettable lemon-lime soda competing against a category it could never lead.\nIn 1968 it renamed the fight: \"The Uncola.\" Not a better cola. Not a cola at all.\nIt didn't change the drink. It changed the shelf in the customer's mind — and became the definitive answer to \"what if I don't want a cola.\"\nColas\n\"The Uncola\"\nAl Ries & Jack Trout, Positioning: The Battle for Your Mind, 1981."},{"n":8,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"KNOWING WHO YOU'RE REALLY FOR — 2007\nPeople camped outside Apple stores for days. Most people didn't buy an iPhone for another two years.\n2.5%\nInnovators\n13.5%\nEarly\nAdopters\n34%\nMajority\nLate\n16%\nLaggards\nThe campers were innovators — 2.5% of any market, thrilled by newness itself. The iPhone didn't win the mainstream on day one. It won them over one segment at a time, each with a different reason to care.\nEverett Rogers, Diffusion of Innovation, 1962."},{"n":9,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"Four stories. Four habits of mind.\nUSER PERSPECTIVE\nIKEA, 1956 — the user's problem was never the table.\nSEGMENTATION iPhone, 2007 — you win a market one segment at a time.\nPOSITIONING\n7 Up, 1968 — own a space no one else has claimed.\nVALUE MIGRATION\nKodak, 1975–2012 — value moves; refusing to follow costs everything.\nMARKETING & BRANDING\nApple, 1997 — sell the belief; let the product earn the rest.\nDATA-DRIVEN DECISIONS\nBooking.com — the opinion is a hypothesis; behavior is the answer.\nCraft builds the product. Mindset decides which product was worth building."},{"n":10,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"SEEING THROUGH THE USER'S EYES — 1956\nA designer tried to fit a table into his own car. It didn't fit — so he took the legs off.\nGillis Lundgren worked for a small Swedish furniture company. Loading a table for a catalogue shoot, he found it too bulky for his car boot. Unscrewing the legs solved it in a minute — and the minute stayed with him.\nHe hadn't set out to invent anything. He'd just refused to accept \"it doesn't fit\" as someone else's problem to solve later.\nFlat-pack furniture — a whole industry, from a car boot that was too small.\nThe user's problem was never the table. It was the car.\nGillis Lundgren, IKEA, 1956 — origin of the flat-pack concept."},{"n":11,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"WHERE THE VALUE GOES NEXT — 1975 TO 2012\nKodak's own engineer built the first digital camera. Kodak buried it to protect film.\nFilm cameras the profitable business, 1975\nDigital & phone cameras where the value moved\nIn 1975, Steven Sasson demonstrated a working digital camera inside Kodak's own labs. Kodak's leadership saw a threat to film profits, not a future business, and shelved it.\nKodak\n2012\nFiled for bankruptcy — 37 years after that lab demo\nAdrian Slywotzky, Value Migration, 1996."},{"n":12,"w":1600,"h":900,"text":"ONE LAST THOUGHT\nKodak's engineer built the future first. His own company still lost it.\nIf the smartest people in the room aren't enough to save a company from its own mindset — what makes you think yours will be different?"}]}]$aiwjson$::jsonb,
